Quickstart
----------

* :ref:`Install native dependencies <inst_native>`: For Debian-based systems, this would be ``apt update && apt install curl git build-essential python3-dev pkg-config qtbase5-dev qtwebengine5-dev libsystemd-dev``
* Clone the repository: ``git clone https://forge.frm2.tum.de/review/frm2/nicos/nicos``
* :ref:`Install uv <inst_uv>`: ``curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh``
* :ref:`Run the demonstration setup <inst_demo>`: ``uv run --extra gui nicos-demo``

Requirements
------------

This section details the dependencies of NICOS. For managing and installing
dependencies, please see the section on :ref:`using uv <inst_uv>`.

If not mentioned otherwise, Python packages are available from PyPI
(https://pypi.python.org/pypi).

* At least Python 3.9

* Package management:

For managing the installation of NICOS and its dependencies, uv should be
installed (available from
https://docs.astral.sh/uv/getting-started/installation/).

You can also use uv to manage the installed Python version(s). See uv's
documentation for details: https://docs.astral.sh/uv/

* NICOS specific Python packages (available from http://forge.frm2.tum.de/simple):

  - nicos-pyctl
  - nicos-quickyaml

* Required Python packages:

  - numpy
  - pyzmq version >= 2
  - rsa
  - tomlkit >= 0.8.0
  - lttb version >= 0.2.2

* For the provided SysV compatible init script and nicos-demo:

  - psutil version >= 0.4 (recommended >= 2.0)

* For running on Linux under systemd:

  - python-systemd

* For hardware access (optional):

  - PyTango 9.x (for TANGO interface, needs TANGO libraries)
  - EPICSv3 and pyepics or p4p/caproto (for Epics interface)
  - a working installation of omniORBpy 4.2.X (http://omniorb.sourceforge.net/) (for CARESS interface)
  - pyserial (for direct serial line communication)

* Optional for the basic system:

  - scipy (for data fitting)
  - docutils (for formatting interactive help nicely)
  - matplotlib (for resolution plots)
  - gnuplot (for plots in the electronic logbook)
  - astropy (for the handling of FITS formatted files)
  - ldap3 (for ldap authentication)
  - sendsms tool to use the SMS notifier (See: http://smslink.sourceforge.net/)
  - keyring, keyrings.alt, pycryptodomex for encrypted password storage (note: using
    the keystore commandline program may require additional system dependent
    dependencies)
  - requests-oauthlib to use the OAuth authentication
  - slackclient python package to use the Slack notifier
  - opencv python package for the sharpness calculation of an image

* For the GUI and status monitor:

  - PyQt5 (^5.13) or PyQt6 (^6.3.1)
  - PyQtWebEngine (^5.13) or PyQt6-WebEngine (^6.3.1)
  - gr (optional, for plots, see: http://gr-framework.org/c.html#linux-packages)
  - QScintilla-python (optional, for the script editor component)
    (not installable with pip)
  - scipy (optional, for fitting)
  - cfitsio (optional, required by nicos-livewidget, this not a Python package)
  - python-redmine (optional, for the bug reporting dialog)
  - sshtunnel (optional, for the ssh tunneling between GUI and server)
  - dvipng (optional, for the hklplot command, this is not a Python package)

* For development (running tests, generating documentation):

  - pytest
  - coverage (optional)
  - sphinx (for generating doc)
  - pyftpdlib

* Under Windows:

  - pyreadline (optional, for the console to work)
  - colorama (optional, for colored console output)

Detailed version requirements can always be found in the pyproject.toml
file in the root of the source tree.


.. _inst_uv:

Installing via ``uv``
---------------------

The NICOS package, its dependencies and even Python itself can be installed via
the uv management tool::

     uv sync --all-packages

This will create a virtual environment inside the source repository (./.venv)
and download and set up (if needed) a Python interpreter. Into the virtual
environment, uv installs all needed dependencies and all NICOS sub-packages.

It is possible that some of the dependencies require a build step: Should you
receive any error messages during this stage, please refer to the section below
on how to install any necessary native libraries on your platform.

You can install specific sub-packages by explicitly selecting them instead::

     uv sync --package nicos-demo

If you would like to use optional components, you can select them via their
respective group using the `--extra` argument. For the GUI and optional groups,
for example, you can specify::

     uv sync --extra gui --extra opt

Other extras are defined in the pyproject.toml file in the root of the
repository.

By default, uv installs the NICOS packages as "editable" meaning that
the installed packages reflect any modifications of the source code inside the repository. 


Instructions for installing uv be found at
https://docs.astral.sh/uv/getting-started/installation/

.. _inst_native:

Native packages to install for common distributions
---------------------------------------------------

While uv will install all necessary Python packages, some require native
libraries to be present on the system. These can be installed via the operating
system's package manager.

GR runtime packages (`gr` and `python-gr`) are available in the repositories
linked at:
https://software.opensuse.org/download.html?project=science:gr-framework&package=gr

Generic Debian/Ubuntu
^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^

Please add the local repository via:

     echo deb [trusted=yes] https://forge.frm2.tum.de/repos distro/| sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/mlz.list
     apt update

where 'distro' stands for:

     - focal (Ubuntu 20.04)
     - jammy (Ubuntu 22.04)
     - bullseye (Debian 11)
     - bookworm (Debian 12)
     - trixie (Debian 13)

* Basic system::

     apt-get install git build-essential python3-dev pkg-config

* Optional components::

     apt-get install libsystemd-dev frappy-core

* GUI and status monitor::

     apt-get install qtbase5-dev qtwebengine5-dev
     apt-get install dvipng texlive-{latex-extra,fonts-recommended} cm-super

* GR library repository

     To add the GR repository the gpg and curl tools are needed

     apt-get install gpg curl
